{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789322673,"nonce":"yI9BjQsSvcbBlDIYuDnATNevqdc=","title":"OSR: what the implementation does not cover, measured against the sibling runtimes","message":"What is there: a Cranelift tier-0 frame transfers into tier-1 or tier-2 code (Cranelift or LLVM) at a loop header through a frame of live-ins; the back edge loads a helper slot and branches; a loop-bearing function asks for promotion on entry; helpers are generation-suffixed for reload; layouts reject external entries, too many live-ins, unsupported instruction kinds and destination-returning functions. Fixed alongside this issue: aggregates travel as pointers (03eba6e), indirect callees and closures are uses, `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR`.\n\nCompared with the other three implementations of the same idea:\n\n1. No transfer out of the interpreter. Tier 0 of `InterpRuntime` is the bytecode interpreter, and it has no back-edge exit into native code, so a function called once with a long loop stays interpreted for the whole run. Both interpreter-first runtimes among the siblings transfer interpreter frames (registers marshalled into a compiled loop-header entry, the interpreter frame parked), and one of them also enters recursion-shaped loopless functions at any single-predecessor block.\n\n2. Probes exist only in tier-0 code. A frame already running tier-1 Cranelift never transfers into tier 2, so LLVM is reached only by functions that are called again after promotion.\n\n3. No way to observe a transfer but the trace print. The IR-level sibling keeps a per-site transfer counter generated code bumps (`transfer_count`, `transfers_taken`), which is what lets a benchmark or test assert that a transfer happened rather than that a helper was published. An A/B where nothing transferred looks like one where the transfer was free.\n\n4. The helper is a codegen mode of each backend (a synthetic prologue in `cranelift_backend.rs` and again in `llvm_backend.rs`), not an IR function. The IR-level sibling builds the variant as an ordinary function (region pruned, prologue block loading the frame, entry edge into the header phis) that every backend then compiles like any other, and it gets the module's optimization passes for free. The aggregate-copy defect existed twice here because of the duplication.\n\n5. Rejections it lacks: a header that is the entry block (the helper would have no parameters to read), and a use of a value nothing defines (dropped silently today, caught only if the helper compile fails).\n\n6. Nothing about trap or handler regions: a loop inside an `Invoke` region or under an effect handler is analysed like any other. Effects reject through the instruction subset; landing pads do not. The Python frontend's pending-exception model is unaffected; ZynML `try`/`with` loops are the case to test.\n\nNot gaps here: address-taken stack slots stay valid because the tier-0 frame stays alive under the helper call; there is no collector to root the frame for.","files":null}]}